.Sh INDEXES ON A HYPERTABLE
Core's own
.Cm create_index ,
.Cm drop_index ,
.Cm add_unique_constraint
and
.Cm add_primary_key
ask this module how an index may be built and dropped on a table, and it answers
for a hypertable with facts measured on both editions:
.Bl -bullet
.It
.Ic CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY
is refused:
.Qq hypertables do not support concurrent index creation .
.Ic DROP INDEX CONCURRENTLY
is refused too.
.It
.Ic WITH (timescaledb.transaction_per_chunk)
builds one chunk at a time, each in its own transaction, holding
.Va ShareLock
on that chunk only \(em but not inside a transaction block, and not for a
UNIQUE index
.Pq Qq cannot use timescaledb.transaction_per_chunk with UNIQUE or PRIMARY KEY .
A failure partway leaves the index INVALID with some chunks built, which is the
shape of a failed concurrent build, and is verified and recovered the same way.
.It
The parent's
.Va relpages
and
.Va reltuples
are 0; the data is in the chunks.
The size and row count come from
.Fn hypertable_size
and
.Fn approximate_row_count .
.It
On 2.30 and later, for a hypertable without the columnstore: an index can be
created
.Ic ON ONLY
the hypertable, which reads no chunk, and
.Ic CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY
then works on each chunk directly.
A chunk created afterwards gets the index by itself, and TimescaleDB counts the
ones built by hand as the hypertable index's own: the planner uses them, and
dropping the hypertable's index removes them.
That last point is what was measured, not something its documentation
promises.
.El
.Pp
The per-chunk option is not as gentle as it reads.
It holds a write-blocking lock on one chunk after another, and a write that
does not name the time column has to lock every chunk, so it waits behind each
in turn: found in the field, updates by id stopped for the whole build.
Measured on a 1.2 GB hypertable of 19 chunks, the per-chunk option took 4.6 s
and an update by id waited up to 3 491 ms; the index on the parent only and then
each chunk concurrently took 2.5 s, with the worst update by id at 43 ms and
the worst insert at 42 ms.
.Pp
So core plans, for an index on a hypertable:
a plain build when the data is small and nothing waits, as for any table;
otherwise, where the module offers it, the index on the parent only and then a
concurrent build of each chunk, a step and a validity check apiece, resumable
\(em a chunk that has its index is left alone, one whose build failed is
dropped and built again; where it does not \(em the columnstore, or a release
before 2.30, neither of which has been measured \(em the per-chunk option; and,
for a UNIQUE index or constraint that is not small, the plain build anyway
\(em the only one that exists \(em with a warning
that writes to the hypertable and every chunk wait for the whole build.
A drop is always a plain
.Ic DROP INDEX .
The step records
.Va index_build_by
as
.Cm timescaledb .
.Sh CONSTRAINTS WITH THE COLUMNSTORE
On any other table
.Cm set_not_null ,
.Cm add_check_constraint
and
.Cm add_foreign_key
add the constraint
.Ic NOT VALID
and validate it in a second step, under a lock that blocks neither reads nor
writes.
On a hypertable with the columnstore enabled that second step is refused
.Pq Qq operation not supported on hypertables that have columnstore enabled ,
after the first has committed.
.Pp
So there the statement that adds the constraint validates it too: one step, in
its own transaction.
Measured on 2.30.2 and 2.28.3 with 137 of 140 chunks converted, each of the
three reads the converted chunks and fails on a violating row that exists only
in one, and the dry run, which executes the step, reports it.
.Pp
What it costs is the lock, held for the whole scan:
.Bl -bullet
.It
.Cm add_check_constraint
and
.Cm set_not_null :
.Va AccessExclusiveLock
on the hypertable and every chunk.
Reads and writes block.
.Cm set_not_null
is a bare
.Ic SET NOT NULL ;
no temporary check is added.
.It
.Cm add_foreign_key :
.Va ShareRowExclusiveLock
on the hypertable and every chunk, and on the referenced table.
Writes block; reads do not.
.El
.Pp
The step states the lock and the size it scans, records
.Va validated_in_one_step_by ,
and the plan carries a warning to schedule it for a quiet window.
This applies as well when the columnstore is enabled by an earlier intent of
the same specification.
A hypertable without the columnstore keeps the two-step recipe.
So does a foreign key on another table that references such a hypertable:
there
.Ic VALIDATE CONSTRAINT
runs on the referencing table, and it reads the converted chunks (measured).
.Sh READINGS
Taken only where the extension is installed in the target database.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Va license , version , functions
.Va timescaledb.license ;
the extension version; which of the functions this module calls exist.
.It Va hypertables
For each: its partitioning columns and the time interval, chunk counts, whether
the columnstore is enabled and how it segments and orders, and its size and
approximate row count.
.It Va policies
Columnstore, retention and refresh jobs, by the hypertable or continuous
aggregate they act on, with their intervals and whether each is scheduled or
paused.
.It Va caggs
Continuous aggregates, and the hypertable each reads.
.El
.Sh A POLICY CHANGED BY HAND
A policy is a job TimescaleDB keeps as a row, and anyone with the rights can
change it with no migration involved:
.Fn remove_retention_policy ,
a second
.Fn add_retention_policy
with another interval,
.Fn alter_job
to pause it.
A retention policy that quietly stopped, or started dropping sooner, is worth
knowing about.
.Pp
Each policy step records what it declared, applied or found already so, and
the ledger keeps it.
On any plan against the database \(em not only one that touches the policy
\(em the newest applied step for each policy is compared with TimescaleDB's
jobs, and a difference is shown as a
.Ql note:
line: the policy is no longer there, exists again after a specification removed it,
has another interval, or its job is paused.
Like every advisory it is shown and not part of
.Va planDigest
.Pq Xr pg_laswell_mcp 1 , Sx planMigration .
It needs a ledger this role can read, and looks at the newest 500 applied
TimescaleDB steps.
Steps applied by a release before 0.1.3-alpha5 recorded no declaration and are
passed over.
.Sh REFUSALS
Of core kinds, on a hypertable \(em including one made a hypertable earlier in
the same specification:
.Bl -bullet
.It
A UNIQUE index, primary key or unique constraint that leaves out a partitioning
column.
.It
.Cm alter_column_type
once a chunk is in the columnstore
.Pq Qq operation not supported on hypertables with compressed chunks .
.El
.Pp
Not refused, and caught by the dry run, which executes it: a column added with a
non-constant default to a hypertable with the columnstore
.Pq Qq cannot add column with non-constant default expression .
